  "prompt_text": "RESEARCH QUESTION:\nWhy do nominally identical 3D-printed polymer tensile coupons show persistent strength differences?\n\nTARGET HYPOTHESIS TO ASSESS:\nProcess-temperature variation explains most of the persistent tensile-strength difference.\n\nMETHODOLOGICAL FRAMING:\nUse a falsification-first approach as the primary methodological frame for the research plan.\n\nAVAILABLE EVIDENCE MODULE CATALOG:\n- M7: Measurement-system reliability and repeatability\n- M2: Controlled variation of candidate process settings\n- M5: Targeted intervention on the leading candidate factor\n- M1: Repeated baseline production and retest series\n- M8: Robustness across alternative specifications\n- M4: Simple single-factor model check\n- M6: Boundary and subgroup analysis\n- M3: Production-history and lot-context reconstruction\n\nEVIDENCE RESULTS CURRENTLY AVAILABLE:\n- M7: Gauge repeatability is high and destructive-test calibration checks show measurement error is too small to explain the persistent gap.\n- M5: Holding resin handling and machine state fixed while changing temperature narrows but does not eliminate the strength difference.\n- M1: Across six repeated baseline batches, tensile strength remains separated into two persistent clusters. The gap is stable across same-day retests.\n- M3: Most low-strength coupons trace to two resin-storage histories and one printer-maintenance interval; temperature settings overlap substantially across high- and low-strength groups.\n\nReturn only the required structured JSON.\nAllocate exactly 100 total attention points across all 8 module IDs.\nThe weights must sum exactly to 100.\ntop3 must contain exactly 3 distinct module IDs in priority order.\ntarget_support_percent is your current percent support for the target hypothesis.\nconfidence_percent is confidence in that current assessment.\nDo not use external tools. Do not add prose.",
